<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinitarians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1198">1198</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Order of the Most Holy Trinity</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-trinitaria">The Trinitarians</a>, the Order of the Most Holy Trinity and of the Captives, are a Catholic religious order founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1198 by John of Matha and approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>. Its original mission was to ransom Christians held captive by Muslims, and for centuries its friars travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, with ransom money, freeing among many others <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> in 1580.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trinitarians.jpg" alt="Trinitarians" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szczepan1990) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trynitarze.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-trinitaria">The Trinitarians</a>, formally the Order of the Most Holy Trinity and of the Captives, are a mendicant order of the Catholic Church for men, founded at Cerfroid, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in the late twelfth century and approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> on 17 December 1198. Papal documents name the founder only as Brother John; tradition identifies him as John of Matha and says the order grew from his vision of Christ with two captives around 1193.</p><p>Trinitarian tradition also counts Felix of Valois as co-founder. The founding intention was the ransom of Christians held captive by Muslims, a consequence of crusading and of piracy along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. Between the eighth and fifteenth centuries raids by militias, bands and armies from both sides were almost an annual occurrence in Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and other coastal provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The order grew quickly from three first houses, at Cerfroid, Planels and Bourg-la-Reine, into a network of houses. The first mission was undertaken by two English friars, who went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1200 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with 186 liberated captives. The first generation counted some fifty foundations, and in northern France the friars were called Mathurins from their base at the church of Saint-Mathurin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, held from 1228.</p><p>The rule required a third of all income to be set aside for ransom. Louis IX installed a house of the order in the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, chose Trinitarians as his chaplains and was accompanied by them on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>; the Master of the Trinity was taken captive with him after the Battle of Al Mansurah.</p><p>The thirteenth century was a time of vitality, and later centuries brought decline in some areas. Reforms followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>, notably the Spanish Discalced Trinitarians under John Baptist of the Conception, and at the beginning of the eighteenth century the order still had 250 houses. Revolution, suppression and civil war later reduced it sharply, but from the late nineteenth century it began to grow again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The habit was white, bearing a cross of red and blue. The rule approved in 1198 was revised in 1267 and 1631. Each local community served its area through hospitality, care of the sick and poor, churches and education, and the friars later took on evangelisation. The wider Trinitarian family includes priests, brothers, cloistered nuns, several congregations of sisters and lay tertiaries, found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Their patroness is Our Lady of Good Remedy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trinitarians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Captives were redeemed through religious orders such as the <b>Trinitarians</b> and Mercedarians, who ransomed Miguel de Cervantes in 1580 after five years in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
